(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["sorting",{"l:sorting/merge-sort-merging-stability-and-inversions":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Merge sort splits the list in half, sorts each half recursively and merges the two sorted halves in one linear pass. Each level of the recursion merges n elements in total and there are log2 n levels, so it is \u003cb>O(n log n) in every case\u003c/b>, with O(n) extra memory. Taking from the left half on ties makes it \u003cb>stable\u003c/b>, and the merge is the moment to count anything that compares the two halves, such as inversions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>The merge invariant.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>out\u003c/code> holds the i + j smallest elements of \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>, in order, and \u003ccode>left[i]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>right[j]\u003c/code> are the smallest not yet taken. Taking the smaller of those two keeps the invariant. On a tie, take from the left: equal items then keep their original order, which is what \u003cb>stable\u003c/b> means and what multi-pass sorts rely on.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Cost.\u003c/b> T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n). The recursion tree has about log2 n levels and each level does O(n) work, so O(n log n) whatever the input. A merge of sizes p and q makes between min(p, q) and p + q - 1 comparisons. Extra memory is O(n) for the merged lists plus an O(log n) stack, so recursion depth is never a problem in Python. The slices \u003ccode>a[:mid]\u003c/code> copy, but that is O(n) per level, the same order as the merge.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Counting during the merge.\u003c/b> When \u003ccode>right[j]\u003c/code> is taken while \u003ccode>left[i:]\u003c/code> remains, it is smaller than all \u003ccode>len(left) - i\u003c/code> of those, and each such pair is an \u003cb>inversion\u003c/b> (i &lt; j with a[i] &gt; a[j]) that crosses the halves. Inversions inside each half are counted by the recursive calls, so the total costs O(n log n). Insertion sort performs exactly one shift per inversion, so this number is its work. When the counting condition differs from the merge order, such as reverse pairs with a[i] &gt; 2 a[j], count in a separate two-pointer pass over the two sorted halves \u003ci>before\u003c/i> merging them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>In real systems.\u003c/b> Merging works on streams read front to back, which is why sorts of data bigger than memory are usually external merge sorts. PostgreSQL's tuplesort sorts work_mem-sized batches in memory, writes them as sorted runs to temporary files and merges the runs with a balanced k-way merge (before version 15 it used polyphase merge, designed for tape drives). CPython's list.sort is a merge sort of natural runs (see the Timsort card), and bottom-up merging, widths 1, 2, 4 and so on, needs no recursion at all.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def merge(left, right):\n    \"\"\"Merge two sorted lists; stable because ties take from the left.\"\"\"\n    out, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i &lt; len(left) and j &lt; len(right):\n        if right[j] &lt; left[i]:            # strictly smaller: only then take from the right\n            out.append(right[j])\n            j += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(left[i])\n            i += 1\n    out.extend(left[i:])\n    out.extend(right[j:])\n    return out\n\n\ndef merge_sort(a):\n    if len(a) &lt;= 1:\n        return a[:]\n    mid = len(a) // 2\n    return merge(merge_sort(a[:mid]), merge_sort(a[mid:]))\n\n\ndef sort_count(a):\n    \"\"\"Return (sorted copy of a, number of pairs i &lt; j with a[i] &gt; a[j]).\"\"\"\n    if len(a) &lt;= 1:\n        return a[:], 0\n    mid = len(a) // 2\n    left, x = sort_count(a[:mid])\n    right, y = sort_count(a[mid:])\n    out, i, j, cross = [], 0, 0, 0\n    while i &lt; len(left) and j &lt; len(right):\n        if right[j] &lt; left[i]:\n            cross += len(left) - i        # right[j] is below every item in left[i:]\n            out.append(right[j])\n            j += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(left[i])\n            i += 1\n    return out + left[i:] + right[j:], x + y + cross\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The last merge of \u003ccode>sort_count([3, 5, 8, 1, 5, 6, 9])\u003c/code>: halves [3, 5, 8] and [1, 5, 6, 9] are already sorted. The left 5 is marked 5L to show stability.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right[0] = 1 &lt; left[0] = 3: take 1; it is below all three left items, so cross += 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left    \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>  5L  8          right   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  5  6  9\nout     1                     cross = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">3 &lt;= 5: take 3 from the left\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left    3  \u003cmark>5L\u003c/mark>  8          right   1  \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>  6  9\nout     1  3                  cross = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">5L vs 5: equal, so take from the LEFT first (stability): 5L\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left    3  5L  \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>          right   1  \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>  6  9\nout     1  3  5L              cross = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right 5 &lt; left 8: take 5; only 8 remains on the left, so cross += 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left    3  5L  \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>          right   1  5  \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>  9\nout     1  3  5L  5           cross = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right 6 &lt; 8: take 6, cross += 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left    3  5L  \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>          right   1  5  6  \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>\nout     1  3  5L  5  6        cross = 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">8 &lt;= 9: take 8; the left is empty, so append the rest of the right\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left    (done)                right   (done)\nout     1  3  5L  5  6  8  9  cross = \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>: (3,1) (5,1) (8,1) (8,5) (8,6)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Writing \u003ccode>if left[i] &lt; right[j]: take left else take right\u003c/code> takes from the right on ties: still sorted, no longer stable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Counting reverse pairs with the merge comparison itself: the merge orders by a[i] &gt; a[j], the question asks a[i] &gt; 2 a[j], so you miss pairs. Count first, then merge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Building many tiny lists is slow in CPython; for speed, merge by index into one preallocated buffer, or let \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> do the work when you do not need the counts.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the inversion count only add \u003ccode>len(left) - i\u003c/code> when an element comes from the right, and nothing when one comes from the left?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An element taken from the left is no larger than everything left in \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>, so it forms no crossing inversion with them. An element taken from the right is strictly smaller than every remaining left element, each of which sits earlier in the array: exactly \u003ccode>len(left) - i\u003c/code> inversions.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to merge stably and how to piggyback cross-half counts on merge sort's O(n log n) passes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:sorting/partitioning-and-quicksort-lomuto-hoare-and-thre":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Partitioning rearranges an array around a pivot so that smaller items come first, in O(n) time and O(1) space. Quicksort partitions and recurses on both sides: O(n log n) expected with a random pivot, O(n^2) when the pivots are consistently bad. A \u003cb>three-way\u003c/b> partition (less, equal, greater) makes duplicates harmless, and recursing into the smaller side first keeps the stack at O(log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Lomuto\u003c/b> uses the last element as the pivot and keeps three regions: a[lo:i] &lt; pivot, a[i:j] &gt;= pivot, a[j:hi] unknown. Each step examines a[j]; if it is smaller it is swapped to position i and the first region grows. Finally the pivot is swapped into position i, its sorted place. Easy to prove, but it swaps a lot, and on all-equal input every split is (n - 1, 0): O(n^2) even with a random pivot.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Hoare\u003c/b> walks two indices inward: i stops at an item &gt;= pivot, j at an item &lt;= pivot, and they swap. It returns j with a[lo..j] &lt;= pivot &lt;= a[j + 1..hi], but the pivot is not necessarily at j, so recurse on [lo, j] and [j + 1, hi]. It does about three times fewer swaps than Lomuto, and equal keys stop both scans, so all-equal input splits evenly.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Three-way (Dijkstra's Dutch national flag)\u003c/b> keeps a[lo:lt] &lt; p, a[lt:i] == p, a[i:gt] unknown and a[gt:hi] &gt; p. Every key equal to the pivot is finished in this pass and never touched again, so a list of few distinct values sorts in close to linear time.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Cost.\u003c/b> With a random pivot, quicksort makes about 2n ln n, roughly 1.39 n log2 n, comparisons on average. The worst case is O(n^2): a first- or last-element pivot on sorted input, or Lomuto on equal keys. Each partition pass is O(n); recursing into the smaller part and looping on the larger bounds the stack at log2 n frames.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Production quicksorts are hybrids.\u003c/b> GCC's \u003ccode>std::sort\u003c/code> (introsort) takes the median of three elements as the pivot, leaves pieces of at most 16 elements for later, switches a range to heapsort once the recursion depth passes 2 log2 n, and finishes with one insertion sort pass over the whole array. The standard requires O(n log n) comparisons in the worst case (defect report LWG 713; average-case used to be enough, which allowed plain quicksort), and LLVM's libc++ met that only in LLVM 14. Go 1.19 moved to pattern-defeating quicksort, and Rust's \u003ccode>sort_unstable\u003c/code> uses ipnsort, a quicksort with a heapsort fallback.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\n\ndef lomuto(a, lo, hi):\n    \"\"\"Partition a[lo..hi] around the pivot a[hi]; return the pivot's final index.\"\"\"\n    pivot, i = a[hi], lo\n    for j in range(lo, hi):              # a[lo:i] &lt; pivot &lt;= a[i:j]\n        if a[j] &lt; pivot:\n            a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]\n            i += 1\n    a[i], a[hi] = a[hi], a[i]\n    return i\n\n\ndef hoare(a, lo, hi):\n    \"\"\"Partition a[lo..hi] (lo &lt; hi); return p with a[lo..p] &lt;= pivot &lt;= a[p+1..hi].\"\"\"\n    pivot = a[(lo + hi) // 2]            # never the last element, or it can loop forever\n    i, j = lo - 1, hi + 1\n    while True:\n        i += 1\n        while a[i] &lt; pivot:\n            i += 1\n        j -= 1\n        while a[j] &gt; pivot:\n            j -= 1\n        if i &gt;= j:\n            return j\n        a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]\n\n\ndef partition3(a, lo, hi, pivot):\n    \"\"\"Rearrange a[lo:hi] into &lt; pivot, == pivot, &gt; pivot; return (lt, gt).\"\"\"\n    lt, i, gt = lo, lo, hi\n    while i &lt; gt:\n        if a[i] &lt; pivot:\n            a[lt], a[i] = a[i], a[lt]\n            lt += 1\n            i += 1\n        elif a[i] &gt; pivot:\n            gt -= 1\n            a[gt], a[i] = a[i], a[gt]\n        else:\n            i += 1\n    return lt, gt\n\n\ndef quicksort(a, lo=0, hi=None):\n    \"\"\"Sort a[lo:hi] in place: random pivot, three-way partition, small side first.\"\"\"\n    if hi is None:\n        hi = len(a)\n    while hi - lo &gt; 1:\n        lt, gt = partition3(a, lo, hi, a[random.randrange(lo, hi)])\n        if lt - lo &lt; hi - gt:\n            quicksort(a, lo, lt)         # recurse on the smaller side...\n            lo = gt                      # ...and loop on the larger one\n        else:\n            quicksort(a, gt, hi)\n            hi = lt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Lomuto on [7, 2, 9, 4, 3, 8, 5] with pivot 5. The bottom row shows the regions: \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> below the pivot, \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code> at least the pivot, \u003ccode>?\u003c/code> not yet seen, \u003ccode>p\u003c/code> the pivot.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">j = 0: 7 &gt;= 5, leave it; the &gt;= region grows to [0, 1)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6\na         \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    2    9    4    3    8    5\n         &gt;=    ?    ?    ?    ?    ?    p\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">j = 1: 2 &lt; 5, swap it to position i = 0; i becomes 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6\na         \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    9    4    3    8    5\n          &lt;   &gt;=    ?    ?    ?    ?    p\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">j = 2: 9 &gt;= 5, leave it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6\na         2    7    \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>    4    3    8    5\n          &lt;   &gt;=   &gt;=    ?    ?    ?    p\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">j = 3: 4 &lt; 5, swap with a[1] = 7; i becomes 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6\na         2    \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>    9    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    3    8    5\n          &lt;    &lt;   &gt;=   &gt;=    ?    ?    p\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">j = 4: 3 &lt; 5, swap with a[2] = 9; i becomes 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6\na         2    4    \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>    7    \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>    8    5\n          &lt;    &lt;    &lt;   &gt;=   &gt;=    ?    p\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">j = 5: 8 &gt;= 5, leave it. The unknown region is empty\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6\na         2    4    3    7    9    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    5\n          &lt;    &lt;    &lt;   &gt;=   &gt;=   &gt;=    p\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Swap the pivot into position i = 3: everything left is smaller, everything right is at least 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6\na         2    4    3    \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>    9    8    7\n          &lt;    &lt;    &lt;    =   &gt;=   &gt;=   &gt;=\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Hoare on the same list (pivot 4, the middle element) needs two swaps and returns 2, leaving [3, 2, 4 | 9, 7, 8, 5].\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hoare's bounds.\u003c/b> Recurse on [lo, p] and [p + 1, hi], not around p as if the pivot sat there; and never take a[hi] as Hoare's pivot: on [1, 2] it returns p = hi and the recursion never shrinks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>First or last element as the pivot\u003c/b> makes sorted input quadratic, and the recursion depth n overflows Python's default limit of 1000 frames after a few thousand items.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two-way partitions on many duplicates\u003c/b> degrade to O(n^2) (Lomuto) or do needless work; use three-way.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quicksort is not stable.\u003c/b> Use it when the order of equal keys does not matter.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">All n keys are equal. What does each scheme do, and what is the total cost of quicksort with it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Lomuto puts everything in the \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code> region, so each pass removes only the pivot: O(n^2). Hoare stops at every element and swaps, splitting near the middle: O(n log n). Three-way puts everything in the equal region in one pass and stops: O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to partition in place with an invariant you can state, and why production quicksorts randomise, use three-way splits and fall back to heapsort.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:sorting/quickselect-the-k-th-element-without-sorting":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>To find the k-th smallest you do not need the whole order: partition once, see which side position k falls on, and continue in that side only. With a random pivot the expected work is about n + n/2 + n/4 + ..., which is O(n); the worst case is O(n^2). In Python, weigh it against \u003ccode>heapq.nsmallest\u003c/code> (O(n log k)) and \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> (O(n log n), but in C).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Narrowing.\u003c/b> Keep [lo, hi) as the slice that still contains position k: everything before lo belongs before position k in sorted order and everything from hi on belongs after it. A three-way partition around p returns lt and gt. If k &lt; lt, continue in [lo, lt); if k &gt;= gt, continue in [gt, hi); otherwise a[k] equals p and you are done. Because it only ever recurses into one side, a loop replaces the recursion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Expected O(n).\u003c/b> A random pivot lands in the middle half of the current slice with probability 1/2, and then the slice shrinks to at most three quarters of its size. So on average every two rounds cut the slice by a quarter, and the total work is a geometric series bounded by a constant times n. Measured for the median of 100,000 random floats, the partitions scanned about 3.4n elements in total, matching the textbook 2(1 + ln 2)n. The worst case, always picking an extreme pivot, is O(n^2); the median-of-medians pivot guarantees O(n) but is slower in practice. C++ offers \u003ccode>std::nth_element\u003c/code>, which in libstdc++ is introselect: quickselect with a heap-based fallback after too many bad splits. Python has no built-in quickselect.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Choosing in Python.\u003c/b> Theory favours quickselect, but constants decide. On CPython 3.12, finding the median of a million random floats took about 1.3 s with the pure-Python quickselect below and about 0.9 s with \u003ccode>sorted(xs)[k]\u003c/code>, because the sort runs in C. \u003ccode>heapq.nsmallest(10, xs)\u003c/code> took 0.03 s.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\u003cth>Best tool\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>k smallest, k small\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heapq.nsmallest(k, xs)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log k); it uses \u003ccode>min\u003c/code> when k = 1 and \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> when k &gt;= n\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>one k-th element, huge n, C or NumPy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>quickselect (\u003ccode>std::nth_element\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>numpy.partition\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) expected\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>one k-th element in pure Python\u003c/td>\u003ctd>usually \u003ccode>sorted(xs)[k]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n), but in C\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>many order queries on fixed data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sort once\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n), then O(1) per query\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>top k of a stream\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a size-k heap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log k) time, O(k) memory\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\n\ndef quickselect(a, k):\n    \"\"\"k-th smallest of a (k = 0 is the minimum) in expected O(n); a is not modified.\n    Uses partition3 from the partitioning card, so duplicates are harmless.\"\"\"\n    a = list(a)\n    lo, hi = 0, len(a)\n    while True:\n        lt, gt = partition3(a, lo, hi, a[random.randrange(lo, hi)])\n        if k &lt; lt:\n            hi = lt\n        elif k &gt;= gt:\n            lo = gt\n        else:\n            return a[k]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The 6th smallest (k = 5) of [7, 2, 9, 4, 6, 1, 8, 3]. The pivots shown are what the random choice happened to pick; the highlighted cells are the pivot's equal block.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pivot 4 on [0, 8): smaller items go left. Equal block [3, 4); k = 5 &gt;= 4, so keep [4, 8)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8\na         3    2    1    \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>    6    8    9    7\n         lo                        k             hi\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pivot 8 on [4, 8): equal block [6, 7); k = 5 &lt; 6, so keep [4, 6)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8\na         3    2    1    4    6    7    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    9\n                             lo    k             hi\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pivot 7 on [4, 6): equal block [5, 6) contains k = 5: the answer is 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0    1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8\na         3    2    1    4    6    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    8    9\n                             lo    k   hi\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three partitions touched 8 + 4 + 2 = 14 cells; a full sort would have ordered all eight.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off by one on k.\u003c/b> \"k-th largest\" (1-based) is index n - k in ascending order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two-way partitions\u003c/b> make all-equal input quadratic; use the three-way version.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing numbers stored as strings\u003c/b> (LeetCode 1985): compare by (length, string), never by string alone (\"9\" &gt; \"10\" as text).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mutating the caller's list\u003c/b>: copy first, as the template does, unless in-place is the point.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need the 100 largest of 10 million scores, once. Which tool and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>heapq.nlargest(100, scores)\u003c/code>: O(n log 100) with a 100-item heap, one pass and almost no memory, and it runs in C. Quickselect would need a copy of all 10 million items and still has to sort the 100 afterwards if order matters.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to select the k-th element in expected linear time, and how to pick the fastest tool in Python for a given n and k.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:sorting/counting-radix-and-bucket-sort-and-the-n-log-n-f":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A sort that only compares items needs at least log2(n!), about n log2 n - 1.44n, comparisons in the worst case, because it must tell all n! orders apart. Sorts that use keys as array indices escape the bound: counting sort is O(n + k) for integer keys below k, radix sort runs a stable counting sort per digit, and bucket sort spreads evenly distributed values into about n buckets.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>The lower bound.\u003c/b> Draw any comparison sort as a binary decision tree: each internal node is one comparison, each leaf one final order. All n! inputs need different leaves, and a tree of height h has at most 2^h leaves, so h &gt;= log2(n!) = n log2 n - n log2 e + O(log n). For n = 100,000 that is about 1.52 million comparisons; CPython's sort used 1.53 million on random floats. The bound says nothing about sorts that never compare.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Counting sort.\u003c/b> Count each key, turn the counts into starting positions with a prefix sum, then place items left to right, advancing each key's position. Placing left to right makes it \u003cb>stable\u003c/b>. O(n + k) time and space: excellent when k is O(n), wasteful when keys reach 10^9.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Radix sort (least significant digit first).\u003c/b> Sort by the last digit, then the next, each pass a stable counting sort. By induction, after pass t the items are ordered by their last t digits, and stability is exactly what keeps the earlier passes' order among equal digits. d digits in base b cost O(d(n + b)): 32-bit integers in base 256 need four passes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Bucket sort and pigeonholes.\u003c/b> n values spread over [lo, hi] go into about n equal-width buckets; if they are roughly uniform each bucket holds O(1) items, so sorting the buckets is O(n) expected. The same pigeonhole idea solves Maximum Gap (164): the largest gap between neighbours is at least (hi - lo) / (n - 1), so it never lies inside a bucket of that width, and only each bucket's minimum and maximum matter.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>When not to.\u003c/b> Floats, strings and composite keys usually want comparisons; a huge key range wastes memory; and in CPython \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> runs in C, so a pure-Python radix sort rarely wins unless the key range is small. In production, NumPy's \u003ccode>np.sort(kind=\"stable\")\u003c/code> uses radix sort for integer data, and PostgreSQL builds its sorted runs with quicksort or radix sort.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def counting_sort(a, key=lambda x: x, k=None):\n    \"\"\"Stable sort of a by integer keys in [0, k). O(n + k) time and space.\"\"\"\n    if k is None:\n        k = max(map(key, a), default=-1) + 1\n    start = [0] * (k + 1)\n    for x in a:\n        start[key(x) + 1] += 1\n    for v in range(k):                    # prefix sums: start[v] = first slot for key v\n        start[v + 1] += start[v]\n    out = [None] * len(a)\n    for x in a:                           # left to right keeps equal keys in order\n        out[start[key(x)]] = x\n        start[key(x)] += 1\n    return out\n\n\ndef radix_sort(a, base=256):\n    \"\"\"Sort non-negative integers: one stable counting sort per base-`base` digit.\"\"\"\n    shift = 1\n    while a and shift &lt;= max(a):\n        a = counting_sort(a, key=lambda x: x // shift % base, k=base)\n        shift *= base\n    return a\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counting sort of five records by their key (0, 1 or 2). The letters show that equal keys keep their input order.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Count the keys, stored one slot to the right: two 0s, one 1, two 2s\u003c/p>\u003cpre>records  2a  0b  2c  1d  0e\nstart    [0, 2, 1, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Prefix sums turn counts into first slots: key 0 starts at 0, key 1 at 2, key 2 at 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>records  2a  0b  2c  1d  0e\nstart    [\u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>, 5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Place 2a at slot start[2] = 3, then start[2] becomes 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>out      [ _,  _,  _, \u003cmark>2a\u003c/mark>,  _ ]\nstart    [0, 2, 4, 5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Place 0b at slot 0 and 2c at slot 4: 2c lands after 2a, as in the input\u003c/p>\u003cpre>out      [\u003cmark>0b\u003c/mark>,  _,  _, 2a, \u003cmark>2c\u003c/mark>]\nstart    [1, 2, 5, 5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Place 1d at slot 2 and 0e at slot 1: done, stable\u003c/p>\u003cpre>out      [0b, \u003cmark>0e\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>1d\u003c/mark>, 2a, 2c]\nstart    [2, 3, 5, 5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Negative keys:\u003c/b> shift by the minimum first (\u003ccode>key = x - lo\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Placing right to left\u003c/b> without iterating the input in reverse breaks stability, and LSD radix sort then gives wrong answers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Radix sort on negative integers\u003c/b> needs the sign handled separately (sort by sign first, or offset).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting sort with k far larger than n\u003c/b> spends its time on the count array. Clamp when the problem allows it: for H-Index (274), any count above n acts like n, so n + 1 buckets suffice.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can no comparison sort beat 3 comparisons in the worst case for 3 items, even though 2 comparisons sometimes suffice?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Three items have 3! = 6 orders, so the decision tree needs 6 leaves; a binary tree of height 2 has at most 4. Some input must take 3 comparisons. (Sorted input can finish after 2, which is why the bound is a worst case.)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why n log n is a floor only for comparison sorts, and how to sort small integer keys in linear time, stably.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:sorting/how-python-and-c-sort-timsort-powersort-and-intr":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>CPython's \u003ccode>list.sort\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> are \u003cb>Timsort\u003c/b>: a stable, adaptive merge sort that finds runs already in order, extends short runs with binary insertion sort, merges neighbouring runs under the \u003cb>powersort\u003c/b> policy (Python 3.11 and later) and \u003cb>gallops\u003c/b> when one run keeps winning. It is O(n log n) in the worst case and O(n) on sorted or reversed input. C++'s \u003ccode>std::sort\u003c/code> is an unstable introsort with an O(n log n) worst case; \u003ccode>std::stable_sort\u003c/code> is a merge sort.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Runs.\u003c/b> One left-to-right pass finds the next run: ascending (non-decreasing) or descending, and a descending run is reversed in place in a way that keeps the sort stable. A run shorter than \u003cb>minrun\u003c/b> is extended to minrun with binary insertion sort. Under 64 items minrun is the whole list; for longer lists it lies between 32 and 64, chosen so runs merge in balanced pairs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Merging.\u003c/b> Only neighbouring runs merge (see the quick check). Pending runs sit on a stack. Since Python 3.11 the merge order follows Munro and Wild's powersort, which is provably near-optimal for the mix of run lengths; earlier versions used Tim Peters' original stack rules. A merge copies only the shorter run to temporary memory, and first binary-searches where B[0] belongs in A and where A[-1] belongs in B, skipping elements already in place.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Galloping.\u003c/b> If one run wins MIN_GALLOP = 7 times in a row, the merge switches to exponential search (look 1, 3, 7, 15, ... ahead, then binary-search) and copies whole chunks. The threshold adapts: it rises when galloping does not pay and falls when it does.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Guarantees.\u003c/b> Only \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> is called, a \u003ccode>key\u003c/code> runs once per element, and \u003ccode>reverse=True\u003c/code> keeps equal items in input order. Comparisons for 100,000 floats on CPython 3.12:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Input\u003c/th>\u003cth>Comparisons\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>already sorted\u003c/td>\u003ctd>99,999\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>reversed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>99,999\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>16 sorted runs, concatenated\u003c/td>\u003ctd>499,985\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ten distinct values\u003c/td>\u003ctd>711,477\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>random\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,529,071 (the lower bound log2(n!) is about 1,516,704)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>C++ and friends.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>std::sort\u003c/code> must make O(n log n) comparisons in the worst case and is not stable; GCC implements it as introsort (see the partitioning card). \u003ccode>std::stable_sort\u003c/code> is a merge sort: O(n log n) comparisons with enough extra memory, O(n log^2 n) without. Rust's \u003ccode>slice::sort\u003c/code> is driftsort (stable) and \u003ccode>sort_unstable\u003c/code> is ipnsort; both run in linear time on fully sorted or reversed input. Go 1.19 rewrote \u003ccode>sort\u003c/code> as pattern-defeating quicksort.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Sorting first.\u003c/b> Once sorted, equal items are adjacent, pairs fall to two pointers, ranges to bisect, and many greedy choices become one scan, as in \u003ccode>largest_perimeter\u003c/code> below.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Counted:\n    \"\"\"Wrap values to count how many comparisons a sort makes.\"\"\"\n    calls = 0\n\n    def __init__(self, v):\n        self.v = v\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        Counted.calls += 1\n        return self.v &lt; other.v\n\n\ndef comparisons(xs):\n    Counted.calls = 0\n    sorted(map(Counted, xs))\n    return Counted.calls\n\n\ndef largest_perimeter(sides):\n    \"\"\"Sort once, then a single scan: the first valid consecutive triple wins.\"\"\"\n    a = sorted(sides, reverse=True)\n    for i in range(len(a) - 2):\n        if a[i] &lt; a[i + 1] + a[i + 2]:\n            return a[i] + a[i + 1] + a[i + 2]\n    return 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>comparisons(range(10))\u003c/code> returns 9: one pass finds a single ascending run and nothing is merged. \u003ccode>comparisons(range(9, -1, -1))\u003c/code> also returns 9: one strictly descending run, reversed in place. A shuffled list of 10 is a single binary insertion sort, about log2(i) comparisons to insert item i: 23 on average and never more than 26 in 20,000 trials, against 45 for plain insertion sort's worst case. At 100,000 items the table above shows the adaptivity: 16 runs cost a third of a random input.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>xs.sort()\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>xs = xs.sort()\u003c/code> loses the list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mixed types (\u003ccode>None\u003c/code> among ints) raise \u003ccode>TypeError\u003c/code>, and NaN breaks the order silently: \u003ccode>sorted([3, float(\"nan\"), 1, 2])\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>[3, nan, 1, 2]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Re-sorting nearly sorted data is cheap, so keeping a list sorted by appending and calling \u003ccode>sort()\u003c/code> again can beat clever structures for small batches; measure before replacing it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>std::sort\u003c/code> is not stable; use \u003ccode>std::stable_sort\u003c/code> when equal keys must keep their order.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does Timsort only merge neighbouring runs, even when merging two non-neighbours first would be cheaper?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Stability. If runs A, B and C all contain an equal key, merging A with C first would place C's copy before B's. Merging neighbours, (A+B)+C or A+(B+C), keeps every equal key in input order.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know what Python's and C++'s built-in sorts really do, what they cost on real inputs, and when to sort first.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:sorting/custom-orders-keys-comparators-and-consistency":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prefer a \u003cb>key function\u003c/b>: it maps each item to a value Python already knows how to order, computed once per item. Multi-key orders are tuple keys (negate numbers to flip a direction) or several stable passes. When the order is only defined pairwise, use \u003ccode>functools.cmp_to_key\u003c/code>, and make sure the comparator is a genuine order (irreflexive, transitive, consistent about ties); otherwise the result is garbage in Python and undefined behaviour in C++.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Keys.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>sorted(xs, key=f)\u003c/code> calls f once per element and sorts the results, which is the old decorate-sort-undecorate idiom built in. Tuples compare left to right, so \u003ccode>key=lambda x: (freq[x], -x)\u003c/code> sorts by increasing frequency and breaks ties by decreasing value. Strings cannot be negated: sort by the secondary key, then by the primary with \u003ccode>reverse=True\u003c/code>; stability keeps the secondary order inside each primary group.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Comparators.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>cmp_to_key(cmp)\u003c/code> wraps each item in an object whose \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> calls \u003ccode>cmp(a, b) &lt; 0\u003c/code>, so every comparison is a Python function call. Sorting 200,000 numeric strings by (length, value) took about 0.63 s with a tuple key and 1.8 s with the equivalent comparator: roughly three times slower.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Consistency.\u003c/b> A valid \"less than\" must be irreflexive (never a &lt; a), transitive (a &lt; b and b &lt; c give a &lt; c), and treat \"neither is less\" as an equivalence that is itself transitive. Common violations: \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code>, NaN among floats (every comparison with NaN is False), \"rock, paper, scissors\" rules, and comparators that depend on outside state. Python never crashes, but the output depends on the input order. In C++ it is undefined behaviour: with GCC's \u003ccode>std::sort\u003c/code>, a \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> comparator on 100 equal ints made the comparator read memory outside the range thousands of times in our test.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Largest number (179).\u003c/b> Put a before b when the string a + b is greater than b + a. This is consistent because, for digit strings, a + b &gt; b + a exactly when a / (10^len(a) - 1) &gt; b / (10^len(b) - 1): it compares the repeating decimals 0.aaa... and 0.bbb..., which are real numbers, so it is transitive. Then handle the all-zeros case, where the answer is \"0\", not \"000\".\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\nfrom functools import cmp_to_key\n\n\ndef concat_order(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Negative when a should come first: a + b forms the larger number.\"\"\"\n    return (b + a &gt; a + b) - (b + a &lt; a + b)\n\n\ndef largest_number(nums):\n    s = \"\".join(sorted(map(str, nums), key=cmp_to_key(concat_order)))\n    return \"0\" if s[0] == \"0\" else s\n\n\ndef frequency_sort(nums):\n    \"\"\"Increasing frequency; equal frequencies by decreasing value.\"\"\"\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    return sorted(nums, key=lambda x: (freq[x], -x))\n\n\ndef sort_by_score_desc_then_name(records):\n    \"\"\"(name, score) pairs: score descending, then name ascending, in two stable passes.\"\"\"\n    by_name = sorted(records, key=lambda r: r[0])\n    return sorted(by_name, key=lambda r: r[1], reverse=True)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Largest number from [3, 30, 34, 5, 9]: each decision compares the two concatenations.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">9 vs 5: \"95\" &gt; \"59\", so 9 goes first\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pair   \"9\" + \"5\" = \u003cmark>95\u003c/mark>    \"5\" + \"9\" = 59\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">3 vs 30: \"330\" &gt; \"303\", so 3 goes before 30, although 3 &lt; 30 as numbers\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pair   \"3\" + \"30\" = \u003cmark>330\u003c/mark>  \"30\" + \"3\" = 303\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">34 vs 3: \"343\" &gt; \"334\", so 34 goes before 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pair   \"34\" + \"3\" = \u003cmark>343\u003c/mark>  \"3\" + \"34\" = 334\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The consistent order puts 9, 5, 34, 3, 30 in sequence\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result \u003cmark>9534330\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A comparator returning a bool (\u003ccode>lambda a, b: a + b &gt; b + a\u003c/code>) is wrong: \u003ccode>cmp_to_key\u003c/code> needs a negative, zero or positive number, and True/False read as 1/0, so \"less than\" never happens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorting digit strings as text: \"10\" &lt; \"9\". Compare by (length, string) for non-negative integers without leading zeros.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Forgetting the all-zeros case in largest number.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Negating a key that is not numeric: use two stable passes instead.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Spot the bug: \u003ccode>sorted(words, key=cmp_to_key(lambda a, b: len(a) &lt;= len(b)))\u003c/code>. What does it actually do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The lambda returns True or False, read as 1 or 0, never negative, so no word is ever \"less\" than another. With nothing to reorder, the output is simply the input order (Timsort sees one long non-decreasing run). Use \u003ccode>key=len\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to express any ordering as a key or a consistent comparator, and how to prove a comparator is a real order.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:sorting/sorting-showdown":"\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Implement\u003c/b> in \u003ccode>sorts.py\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>insertion_sort\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>merge_sort\u003c/code> (stable), \u003ccode>quick\u003c/code> (copy, then the random three-way \u003ccode>quicksort\u003c/code>), \u003ccode>naive_quick\u003c/code> (first element as pivot, two-way, recursive), and \u003ccode>counting(a, k)\u003c/code> for integers in [0, k). Each returns a new sorted list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stress-test first.\u003c/b> For 2,000 random lists of length 0 to 30 with values 0 to 9, assert every function equals \u003ccode>sorted(xs)\u003c/code>. For stability, sort (key, tag) pairs by key with \u003ccode>merge_sort\u003c/code> (wrap them so \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> compares keys only) and check the tags of equal keys stay in input order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inputs.\u003c/b> For each n build four lists of integers below 1,000: random, sorted, reversed, and \"few distinct\" (values 0 to 4).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Time\u003c/b> with the best of three \u003ccode>time.perf_counter()\u003c/code> runs. Use n = 1,000, 2,000, 4,000 for \u003ccode>insertion_sort\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>naive_quick\u003c/code>, and n = 50,000, 100,000, 200,000 for the rest. Print one table: algorithm, input shape, times, and the ratio between successive sizes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursion.\u003c/b> Run \u003ccode>naive_quick(list(range(3000)))\u003c/code> with the default recursion limit. What happens, and why?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Count comparisons\u003c/b> of \u003ccode>sorted()\u003c/code> with the \u003ccode>Counted\u003c/code> wrapper from the Timsort card on the four shapes at n = 100,000, and compare with log2(n!) (\u003ccode>math.lgamma(n + 1) / math.log(2)\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Read\u003c/b> the Intro of listsort.txt and write three sentences explaining your \u003ccode>sorted()\u003c/code> numbers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Exact times depend on the machine; on a busy one take the best of three runs and trust the ratios more than the numbers.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Insertion sort\u003c/b>: roughly 4x per doubling on random, reversed and few-distinct input (quadratic), but almost instant on sorted input, where it is the fastest hand-written sort: one comparison per item.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Merge sort and three-way quicksort\u003c/b>: a little over 2x per doubling (n log n) on every shape. Quicksort is usually faster than merge sort on random data (in place, no list building) and far faster on few-distinct input, where the equal blocks finish in one pass.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Naive quicksort\u003c/b>: fine on random input, quadratic on sorted and reversed input, and at n = 3,000 sorted it raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> (depth about n, default limit 1,000).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting sort\u003c/b> with k = 1,000: linear, and faster than \u003ccode>sorted()\u003c/code> at these sizes because the key range is small.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>sorted()\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: 10 to 20 times faster than your best pure-Python n log n sort on random data, and far faster still on sorted or reversed input. Comparisons: 99,999 for sorted and reversed, about 1.53 million for random against a lower bound of about 1.52 million, and fewer for few-distinct input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The explanation: C code and no interpreter overhead per comparison; natural runs make sorted and reversed input a single pass; and on random data Timsort sits within about 1% of the information-theoretic minimum number of comparisons.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:1122":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reorder a list of non-negative integers using a separate priority list: values named there come first in that exact order, with all their occurrences together. Values absent from the priority list follow in ascending order. For \u003ccode>[2, 1, 2, 3, 1]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[2, 1]\u003c/code>, return \u003ccode>[2, 2, 1, 1, 3]\u003c/code>. The result must preserve the input's multiset.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Input values lie in \u003ccode>0..1000\u003c/code>; priority values are distinct and occur in the input. Repeated input values are separate occurrences. This small, fixed universe makes a count array practical even when the input contains many duplicates. An empty input and an empty priority list are also meaningful extensions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Values are small non-negative integers (at most 1,000) and the target order is given by a second list, with leftovers ascending.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With values this small, do you need comparisons at all? What if you counted each value first?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting, radix and bucket sort\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count arr1 into a 1,001-slot array, emit each arr2 value as many times as counted (zeroing the count), then sweep the remaining values in increasing order. That is counting sort with a custom output order: O(n + V), no comparisons.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + V) time, O(V) space, where V is the value range\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each priority value scan the entire input and emit matches, then sort all unmentioned values. With n items and p priorities this takes O(np + n log n) time and O(n + p) space. Scanning repeatedly rediscovers the same frequencies.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count each input value once. Emit the requested buckets in priority order, clearing each emitted bucket so it cannot appear again. Then sweep the whole universe from low to high. Every occurrence is emitted exactly once: either its bucket was cleared by the priority phase or it remains for the ascending phase.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [2, 1, 2, 3, 1], priority [2, 1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>counts: 1 -&gt; 2, 2 -&gt; 2, 3 -&gt; 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Emit 2 twice, then 1 twice; clear both buckets\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result [2, 2, 1, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sweep remaining buckets ascending; emit 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result \u003cmark>[2, 2, 1, 1, 3]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def relative_sort(values, priority):\n    counts = [0] * 1001\n    for value in values:\n        counts[value] += 1\n    result = []\n    for value in priority:\n        result.extend([value] * counts[value])\n        counts[value] = 0\n    for value, count in enumerate(counts):\n        result.extend([value] * count)\n    return result\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For universe size V=1001, n input values and p priorities, counting, emission and sweeping take O(n + p + V) time. Counts use O(V) auxiliary space and the returned list uses O(n). Neither input is modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Forgetting to clear a priority bucket emits its values twice.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A priority list need not be numerically sorted; only leftovers are.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This array representation assumes the stated non-negative bounded domain; use a map for other values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For an unbounded universe, count with a dictionary and sort only the unmentioned distinct values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the final sweep to descending order if leftovers must be largest first.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1636":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the input numbers ordered by increasing occurrence count. When two different numbers have the same count, put the larger number first. For \u003ccode>[4, 4, -1, -1, 2]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[2, 4, 4, -1, -1]\u003c/code>. Each occurrence appears as many times as it did originally.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counts refer to the original collection, not a changing prefix. Integers may be negative and duplicates count individually. The input may be empty in this implementation. A lexicographic tuple can encode both priorities without a hand-written comparator.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Order numbers by how often they occur, and break ties in the opposite direction from the main order.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can the whole rule be one value per element that Python already orders correctly?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Custom orders: keys and comparators\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count with Counter, then sort with key=lambda x: (freq[x], -x): the tuple compares frequency first, and negating the value flips the tie-break to descending. One key, computed once per element, no comparator.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every element, scan the list to compute its frequency, cache that element's ordering key and then sort. Frequency computation costs O(n²), followed by O(n log n) sorting; the copied output uses O(n) space. Recounting equal numbers is unnecessary.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a frequency map in one pass. Sort each number x by \u003ccode>(frequency[x], -x)\u003c/code>. Python compares the frequency first in ascending order; for equal frequencies, negating x makes the larger original value have the smaller key. The tuple exactly expresses the requested two-stage rule.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [4, 4, -1, -1, 2]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frequencies: 4 -&gt; 2, -1 -&gt; 2, 2 -&gt; 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Keys: 2 -&gt; (1, -2), 4 -&gt; (2, -4), -1 -&gt; (2, 1)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first compare frequencies, then the negated values\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sorted keys give {{[2, 4, 4, -1, -1]}}\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every pair of distinct values is ordered according to its frequency or, on a tie, its descending numeric value. Sorting the occurrences by that key therefore satisfies the whole rule and cannot change their multiplicities.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\ndef frequency_sort_numbers(values):\n    counts = Counter(values)\n    return sorted(values, key=lambda value: (counts[value], -value))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counting takes O(n) expected time, and sorting takes O(n log n) time. The frequency map and sorted output together use O(n) space. The caller's list is unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sorting by \u003ccode>(count, value)\u003c/code> reverses the requested tie-break.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Negating the count instead would sort frequent numbers first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Negative values work because negation reverses their numeric order too.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Order equal-frequency values ascending by replacing \u003ccode>-value\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>value\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a tiny bounded integer domain, bucket distinct values by frequency before emitting them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:976":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose three lengths that form a nondegenerate triangle and return the largest achievable perimeter, or zero when no triple works. For \u003ccode>[2, 1, 2, 4]\u003c/code>, the best valid triple is \u003ccode>(2, 2, 1)\u003c/code>, with perimeter 5. Each chosen length must come from a different input position.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Lengths are positive integers and there are at least three in the original task. This implementation also accepts shorter lists. Once three positive lengths are sorted as a &gt;= b &gt;= c, only the strict inequality b + c &gt; a needs checking; equality is a flat, invalid triangle.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pick three lengths that form a triangle with the largest perimeter.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix the longest side. Which two other sides give it the best chance of satisfying the triangle inequality?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Built-in sorts and sorting first\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort descending. For longest side a[i], the best partners are the next two, a[i+1] and a[i+2]; if a[i] &gt;= a[i+1] + a[i+2], no smaller pair can work, so a[i] can never be the longest side. The first i with a[i] &lt; a[i+1] + a[i+2] gives the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate every triple, check its largest side against the other two, and keep the largest valid sum. This takes O(n³) time and O(1) extra space, but repeats the same comparisons among many closely related triples.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort descending and examine consecutive triples. For any candidate largest side a, the next two available sides are the largest possible partners. If their sum is not greater than a, no other pair below a can make a triangle with it. If they do work, their sum gives the best perimeter among triples with that largest side, and earlier largest sides have already been ruled out. Any later triple has no greater sum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [2, 1, 2, 4]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>descending [4, 2, 2, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Try 4, 2, 2: 2 + 2 = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>invalid, and no smaller partners can rescue 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Try 2, 2, 1: 2 + 1 &gt; 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first valid perimeter \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def largest_perimeter(lengths):\n    ordered = sorted(lengths, reverse=True)\n    for i in range(len(ordered) - 2):\n        if ordered[i + 1] + ordered[i + 2] &gt; ordered[i]:\n            return ordered[i] + ordered[i + 1] + ordered[i + 2]\n    return 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting takes O(n log n) time and the scan O(n). The sorted copy uses O(n) space; the original list is untouched.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use strict inequality; sides summing exactly to the longest are degenerate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Equal lengths can be used if they are separate occurrences.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty, short, and impossible lists return zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the chosen triple rather than just its perimeter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Enumerate all valid triples if the goal is their count, not the maximum.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:912":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the input integers in nondecreasing order without relying on a built-in sorting routine. Keep every occurrence, including duplicates. For \u003ccode>[3, -1, 3, 2]\u003c/code>, return \u003ccode>[-1, 2, 3, 3]\u003c/code>. The implementation returns a new list rather than changing the caller's list.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The sequence can contain negative values, repeated values, already sorted data, or reverse-sorted data. Input sizes can be large enough that quadratic sorting is unsuitable. The task calls for O(n log n) time and linear auxiliary space is available. Empty and single-element lists are natural base cases.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sort an array yourself in O(n log n), without the built-in sort; the tests include sorted and heavily duplicated arrays.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which algorithms keep O(n log n) on sorted input and on arrays where most values are equal? What goes wrong with a first-element pivot?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Partitioning and quicksort\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use quicksort with a random pivot and a three-way partition (equal keys finish in one pass, sorted input is no longer adversarial), recursing on the smaller side; or merge sort, which is O(n log n) on every input at the price of O(n) extra memory.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) expected time, O(log n) extra space (quicksort) or O(n) (merge sort)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeatedly select the minimum remaining value and append it to the result. A linear search for each of n selections costs O(n²) time; a copied collection and output use O(n) space. Insertion sort has the same worst-case time on reverse-sorted input.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort two halves independently and merge their sorted runs. The smaller of the two current heads is the smallest value not yet emitted. Once one run is exhausted, its counterpart's remaining suffix is already ordered. Taking the left value on a tie preserves the relative order of equal keys. Recursion bottoms out at length zero or one, which is already sorted.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The proof is inductive: both recursive results are sorted permutations of their halves. Each merge step removes precisely one smallest remaining element, and appending the remaining suffix preserves order. Thus the merged result is a sorted permutation of the whole input, not merely a list that looks ordered.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [3, -1, 3, 2]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>split [3, -1] and [3, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sort and merge each pair\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left [-1, 3], right [2, 3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Choose -1, then 2, then the left 3; append the last 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result \u003cmark>[-1, 2, 3, 3]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def merge_sort(values):\n    if len(values) &lt;= 1:\n        return list(values)\n    mid = len(values) // 2\n    left = merge_sort(values[:mid])\n    right = merge_sort(values[mid:])\n    result = []\n    i = j = 0\n    while i &lt; len(left) and j &lt; len(right):\n        if left[i] &lt;= right[j]:\n            result.append(left[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            result.append(right[j])\n            j += 1\n    result.extend(left[i:])\n    result.extend(right[j:])\n    return result\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At each of O(log n) levels, splitting and merging process O(n) elements, so time is O(n log n), including sorted and duplicate-heavy inputs. Peak auxiliary space is O(n) for slices, recursive results and the merge output, plus O(log n) stack frames. The returned list is included in that linear bound.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Append both unconsumed suffixes; exactly one may be nonempty when the loop stops.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Taking the right side on equality still sorts integers but loses stability for records.\u003c/li>\u003cli>First-element-pivot quicksort can become quadratic on already sorted data; merge sort does not have that failure mode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Returning the original singleton would alias the caller's list, so the base case copies it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Merge records by a chosen key while preserving the original order of ties.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count cross-half inversions during each merge instead of only sorting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use an iterative bottom-up merge to avoid recursive calls.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:179":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Arrange non-negative integers so that writing their decimal representations consecutively produces the largest possible decimal integer. Return that result as a string, since it can be much larger than an integer machine word. For \u003ccode>[3, 30, 34]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>\"34330\"\u003c/code>. Multiple zeroes should produce the canonical answer \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inputs are non-negative integers, so their decimal forms have no signs. There is at least one element in the original task; this implementation returns \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code> for an empty list as a useful extension. Sorting numbers numerically or sorting their strings lexicographically does not consistently determine the best concatenation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Arrange numbers so that their concatenation is as large as possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For just two numbers, how do you decide which goes first? Is that rule safe to use as a sort order?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Custom orders: keys and comparators\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort the strings with cmp_to_key, putting a before b when a + b &gt; b + a. It is a valid order because it compares the repeating decimals 0.aaa... and 0.bbb..., which is transitive. Return \"0\" when the first string is \"0\".\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) comparisons of O(L) each, where L is the number of digits; O(nL) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try every permutation, concatenate its decimal strings, and retain the greatest result. All candidates have the same total length, so lexical comparison works. The n! arrangements cost O(n! times D) time for D total digits, and O(D + n) working space aside from permutation generation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For two strings a and b, prefer a before b exactly when \u003ccode>a+b\u003c/code> is lexicographically greater than \u003ccode>b+a\u003c/code>. An adjacent swap that violates this rule improves the whole concatenation: the surrounding prefix and suffix are unchanged, and the compared middle strings have equal length.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This comparator is consistent, not merely a local heuristic. Let A and B be the integer values of the strings and let p and q be their lengths. Comparing \u003ccode>a+b\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>b+a\u003c/code> is equivalent to comparing \u003ccode>A/(10**p - 1)\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>B/(10**q - 1)\u003c/code>, the values represented by repeating each string indefinitely. These rational values have a transitive order. Sorting by it leaves no improving adjacent swap; equivalently, any permutation can be transformed into the sorted order without decreasing its concatenation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [3, 30, 34]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>strings \"3\", \"30\", \"34\"\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\"34\" before \"3\": \"343\" &gt; \"334\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\"3\" before \"30\": \"330\" &gt; \"303\"\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Join [\"34\", \"3\", \"30\"]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result \u003cmark>\"34330\"\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from functools import cmp_to_key\n\ndef largest_number(values):\n    parts = [str(value) for value in values]\n\n    def compare(a, b):\n        ab, ba = a + b, b + a\n        return (ba &gt; ab) - (ba &lt; ab)\n\n    parts.sort(key=cmp_to_key(compare))\n    if not parts or parts[0] == \"0\":\n        return \"0\"\n    return \"\".join(parts)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For n numbers with at most L digits each, sorting uses O(n log n) comparisons, each taking O(L) time and temporary space. Conversion and joining take O(nL), so total time is O(n log(n+1) L), with O(nL + n) space for strings, sort keys, and output.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Numeric descending order fails for \u003ccode>3\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>30\u003c/code>; ordinary string order also fails on other prefixes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The comparator must return zero when the two concatenations match, as with \u003ccode>12\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>1212\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Without zero normalization, \u003ccode>[0, 0]\u003c/code> would produce \u003ccode>\"00\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Negative numbers are outside the contract: a minus sign makes this concatenation order invalid.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse the comparator to produce the smallest concatenation, specifying how leading zeroes should be represented.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep original indices with the strings if the chosen arrangement must also be returned.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:451":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rearrange a string so that characters with higher frequencies come before characters with lower frequencies. All occurrences of each character form one group. Any ordering among equally frequent characters is acceptable. For \u003ccode>\"tree\"\u003c/code>, both \u003ccode>\"eert\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>\"eetr\"\u003c/code> are valid because \u003ccode>e\u003c/code> occurs twice and the other characters once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Characters are individual Python string elements, including digits, punctuation, and Unicode code points; case is significant. The output must be a permutation of the input and retain its length. A frequency is an integer from 1 through n, so it can index a bucket array of size n+1. The empty string should return empty.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rearrange the characters of a string by decreasing frequency.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Frequencies are whole numbers no larger than the string's length. Could each frequency be a bucket?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting, radix and bucket sort\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count characters, then put each character into bucket[freq] (a list of lists of size n + 1) and read the buckets from high to low, writing each character freq times. Bucket sort by frequency is O(n); sorting the distinct characters by count is also fine because there are at most a few dozen.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time with buckets, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count characters, then repeatedly scan the distinct characters for the highest remaining count, output its group and remove it. With d distinct characters this costs O(n + d²) time and O(n + d) space. Comparing and sorting the distinct counts is also valid, but takes O(d log d) time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count once, put each distinct character in the bucket indexed by its frequency, and visit buckets from n down to 1. When visiting bucket f, emit each of its characters f times. Every character is placed in exactly one bucket and is emitted with its original multiplicity. Since a higher bucket is processed first, no less frequent group precedes a more frequent group.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Ties need no secondary comparison: their groups have equal frequency, so their order cannot violate the condition. In this implementation insertion order in the frequency map makes the choice deterministic for a given input, but callers should not depend on a particular tie order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input \"tree\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>counts t:1, r:1, e:2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bucket 2 contains e; bucket 1 contains t and r\u003c/p>\u003cpre>emit \"ee\", then the two singleton groups\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">One valid result is {{\"eetr\"}}\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\ndef frequency_sort_characters(text):\n    counts = Counter(text)\n    buckets = [[] for _ in range(len(text) + 1)]\n    for char, frequency in counts.items():\n        buckets[frequency].append(char)\n    groups = []\n    for frequency in range(len(text), 0, -1):\n        for char in buckets[frequency]:\n            groups.append(char * frequency)\n    return \"\".join(groups)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counting, allocating and scanning buckets, placing distinct characters and writing the result all take O(n) expected time. The buckets, groups and output occupy O(n) space. Dictionary operations have their usual expected-time assumption.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Emitting a character only once loses occurrences; repeat it by its bucket index.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Buckets go from high to low, not low to high.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tied frequencies can appear in either order; tests should check the property, not one particular string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Case-distinct characters are distinct; grapheme clusters containing multiple code points are not treated as single characters.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sort distinct characters by count if the number of distinct characters is much smaller than the string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Break ties lexicographically by ordering the characters within each frequency bucket.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:274":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given the citation count of each paper, find the largest integer h such that at least h papers each have at least h citations. For \u003ccode>[3, 0, 6, 1, 5]\u003c/code>, the answer is 3: three papers have at least three citations, but fewer than four have at least four.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Citation counts are non-negative integers and the order of papers is irrelevant. If there are n papers, h cannot exceed n, regardless of how large any citation count is. Therefore counts larger than n can be grouped together without changing any candidate's answer. The empty collection has h-index zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The largest h such that at least h papers have at least h citations each, with citations up to 1,000 and n up to 5,000.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Does it matter whether a paper has n citations or a million?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting, radix and bucket sort\u003c/p>\u003cp>Clamp every count to n and counting-sort into n + 1 buckets. Sweep h from n down to 0, accumulating how many papers have at least h citations; the first h where that total reaches h is the answer. (Sorting descending and finding the last i with c[i] &gt;= i + 1 also works in O(n log n).)\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each candidate h from 0 to n, scan all papers and count those with at least h citations, retaining the largest feasible candidate. This takes O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space. Sorting the counts and scanning is another solution with O(n log n) time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build n+1 buckets, placing each paper in \u003ccode>min(citations, n)\u003c/code>. Sweep candidate h downward from n, accumulating all buckets at or above h. That running total is exactly the number of papers with at least h citations: clamping cannot change whether a count reaches any h &lt;= n.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The first candidate for which the cumulative count reaches h is feasible. Every larger candidate has already failed, so this first success is the largest possible h. Candidate zero always succeeds, even when there are no papers.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, one paper with a thousand citations still contributes only one paper to the cumulative count. The algorithm is counting papers that cross a threshold, not summing citations or finding an average. Many papers with zero citations never help a positive candidate, although they remain included in the total number of papers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [3, 0, 6, 1, 5], n = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>capped buckets: 0:1, 1:1, 3:1, 5:2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">h=5: 2 papers, then h=4: 2 papers\u003c/p>\u003cpre>neither reaches its candidate\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">h=3: cumulative count 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first feasible candidate is \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def h_index(citations):\n    n = len(citations)\n    buckets = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for count in citations:\n        buckets[min(count, n)] += 1\n    at_least = 0\n    for h in range(n, -1, -1):\n        at_least += buckets[h]\n        if at_least &gt;= h:\n            return h\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Filling and sweeping the buckets take O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space. The input is not modified; very large citation counts do not enlarge the bucket array.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Accumulate buckets before checking h, so papers with exactly h citations count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A paper with more than n citations still contributes to every relevant candidate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Zero papers and all-zero citations return zero; negative counts are not part of the contract.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the first successful candidate while sweeping downward, not the number of papers seen.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>If counts are already sorted, find the boundary with binary search instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For repeatedly updated citation counts, maintain cumulative frequencies in an indexed tree.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1985":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the kth largest entry among non-negative integers written as decimal strings. Duplicates occupy separate ranks. For \u003ccode>[\"3\", \"21\", \"100\", \"21\"]\u003c/code> and k=2, return \u003ccode>\"21\"\u003c/code>. Return the original string, not an integer conversion or a distinct-value rank.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n &gt;= 1 strings and \u003ccode>1 &lt;= k &lt;= n\u003c/code>. Each is a canonical decimal representation: \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code> or digits without leading zeroes. Strings can contain far more digits than a fixed-width integer, so comparison should not depend on machine integer size. Under this representation, longer strings represent larger numbers, and equal-length strings compare numerically in lexicographic order.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The k-th largest of numbers given as strings of up to 100 digits, with no leading zeros.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>How do you compare two such numbers without converting them? Do you need the full order to find one position?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Quickselect and top-k choices\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compare by (len(s), s): a longer string is a larger number, and equal lengths compare as text. Then quickselect for index n - k in ascending order (expected O(n) comparisons), or keep a size-k min-heap of those keys, or simply sort with that key.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) expected comparisons of O(L) each with quickselect; O(n log k) with a heap\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compare every pair to count how many entries are greater than each candidate, then locate the kth rank with appropriate handling of duplicates. This takes O(n² L) time for maximum length L. Alternatively sort the whole collection in O(n log n) comparisons, which is simple but retains more ordered information than a single rank needs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a min-heap of at most k tuples \u003ccode>(length, string)\u003c/code>. The tuple is a numeric ordering key under the no-leading-zero contract. If a new key is greater than the heap's minimum, replace that minimum; otherwise discard it. Before the heap is full, insert everything.\u003c/p>\u003cp>By induction the heap holds the largest min(k, processed) entries, including duplicate occurrences. When a full heap sees a larger key, its old minimum is the only retained candidate displaced; a smaller or equal key cannot change the kth value. After all entries, the root's string is the kth largest.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Strings [\"3\", \"21\", \"100\", \"21\"], k=2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>keep keys (1, \"3\") and (2, \"21\")\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">See (3, \"100\"): replace (1, \"3\")\u003c/p>\u003cpre>heap contains \"21\" and \"100\"\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">See another \"21\": it ties the boundary\u003c/p>\u003cpre>root gives \u003cmark>\"21\"\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\ndef kth_largest_string(numbers, k):\n    if not 1 &lt;= k &lt;= len(numbers):\n        raise ValueError(\"k must be between 1 and the number of entries\")\n    heap = []\n    for number in numbers:\n        key = (len(number), number)\n        if len(heap) &lt; k:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, key)\n        elif key &gt; heap[0]:\n            heapq.heapreplace(heap, key)\n    return heap[0][1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(n log(k+1)) heap comparisons in the worst case, each taking O(L) for equal-length strings, for O(n L log(k+1)) time. The heap stores O(k) tuple references; excluding the input strings, auxiliary space is O(k). No numeric conversion or input mutation is needed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Lexicographic ordering alone puts \u003ccode>\"9\"\u003c/code> above \u003ccode>\"10\"\u003c/code>; compare length first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Removing duplicates changes the requested rank.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Leading zeroes invalidate the length argument and would need normalization first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reject k=0 or k&gt;n instead of returning an unrelated root.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sort all strings by \u003ccode>(len(s), s)\u003c/code> when simplicity is more important than top-k storage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use randomized quickselect on these keys for expected O(n) comparisons, with a quadratic worst case.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:164":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the largest difference between adjacent values in the sorted order of an integer list, without actually comparison-sorting it. Return zero for fewer than two values. For \u003ccode>[3, 6, 9, 1]\u003c/code>, sorting would give \u003ccode>[1, 3, 6, 9]\u003c/code>, so the maximum adjacent gap is 3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original task asks for linear time and space and uses non-negative integers; this implementation also supports negative integers. Duplicates are allowed. If all values are equal, the answer is zero. Otherwise the integer range from minimum to maximum and the number of gaps give a lower bound on the maximum gap.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The maximum difference between neighbours in sorted order, required in linear time and space.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With n values between lo and hi, how small can the largest gap possibly be? Could the answer lie inside a narrow bucket?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting, radix and bucket sort\u003c/p>\u003cp>The n - 1 gaps add up to hi - lo, so the largest is at least w = ceil((hi - lo) / (n - 1)). Put each value in bucket (x - lo) // w (about n buckets): two values in the same bucket differ by less than w, so the answer is a jump from one non-empty bucket's maximum to the next non-empty bucket's minimum. Store only each bucket's min and max.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort a copy and scan adjacent differences. This is straightforward and takes O(n log n) time and O(n) space for the copy, but misses the requested linear bound. Comparing every pair is even slower and does not directly tell which values are adjacent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>span = max - min\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>w = ceil(span / (n - 1))\u003c/code>. The n-1 adjacent gaps sum to span, so at least one gap is at least w. Put each value into bucket \u003ccode>(value - min) // w\u003c/code>. Distinct integers within one bucket differ by at most w-1, so a maximum gap cannot lie inside a bucket. It must connect the maximum of one nonempty bucket to the minimum of the next nonempty bucket.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Only the minimum and maximum of each bucket are needed. Sweeping nonempty buckets in increasing order compares exactly those cross-bucket neighbors. There are at most n buckets because \u003ccode>w &gt;= span/(n-1)\u003c/code>, so the bucket holding the maximum has index at most n-1. Duplicate values do not change this argument; when span is zero we return before dividing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [3, 6, 9, 1]: n=4, min=1, max=9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>span=8, width=ceil(8/3)=3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Nonempty buckets: [1,3], [6], [9]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>record only minimum and maximum of each\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Cross gaps 6-3=3 and 9-6=3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>maximum is \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def maximum_gap(values):\n    n = len(values)\n    if n &lt; 2:\n        return 0\n    low, high = min(values), max(values)\n    if low == high:\n        return 0\n    width = (high - low + n - 2) // (n - 1)\n    size = (high - low) // width + 1\n    mins = [None] * size\n    maxs = [None] * size\n    for value in values:\n        index = (value - low) // width\n        if mins[index] is None:\n            mins[index] = maxs[index] = value\n        else:\n            mins[index] = min(mins[index], value)\n            maxs[index] = max(maxs[index], value)\n    best = 0\n    previous_max = None\n    for bucket_min, bucket_max in zip(mins, maxs):\n        if bucket_min is None:\n            continue\n        if previous_max is not None:\n            best = max(best, bucket_min - previous_max)\n        previous_max = bucket_max\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Finding extrema, distributing values and scanning at most n buckets take O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space under constant-time integer arithmetic. The input is unchanged. For arbitrary-precision integers, arithmetic also depends on the number of digits.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Floor division without rounding up can create too many buckets or fail the within-bucket gap proof.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>, not zero, to mark empty buckets: zero and negative values are legitimate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Skip empty buckets, comparing the nearest nonempty neighbors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Handle all-equal input before computing the bucket index, avoiding division by zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the two values on either side of the largest gap as well as its size.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For floating-point inputs, define precision and bucket boundaries carefully before adapting the proof.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:493":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count index pairs \u003ccode>(i, j)\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>i &lt; j\u003c/code> for which the value at i is strictly greater than twice the value at j. For \u003ccode>[1, 3, 2, 3, 1]\u003c/code>, the answer is 2: the two occurrences of 3 before the final 1 each form a qualifying pair with it. Return a count, not the pairs themselves, and do not reorder the caller's input.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inputs can contain negative numbers, zero, duplicates and large-magnitude integers; the original task allows tens of thousands of entries. A quadratic pair scan is too slow at that scale. The inequality is strict, and the order of indices matters even though the values will be sorted internally. Python integers avoid overflow in both doubling and the count; fixed-width implementations must widen before multiplying.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For negative values, a value can form a qualifying pair with an equal value: for example \u003ccode>-1 &gt; 2 * -1\u003c/code>. Consequently, shortcuts that assume a reverse pair is always an ordinary inversion are incorrect. Empty and singleton inputs have no pairs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count pairs i &lt; j with nums[i] &gt; 2 * nums[j] in an array of up to 50,000 values.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Merge sort already brings the left half and the right half together in sorted order. What can you count cheaply when both halves are sorted?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Merge sort and counting during the merge\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inside merge sort, before merging two sorted halves, sweep j over the right half while a pointer over the left half finds the first left value &gt; 2 * right[j]; every left value from there on pairs with right[j]. The pointer only moves forward, so counting is O(n) per level; then merge normally. Do not count inside the merge loop: its order is by a[i] &gt; a[j], not 2 * a[j].\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use two nested loops over i and j, with j starting at i+1, and increment the answer whenever the inequality holds. This directly reflects the definition and costs O(n²) time with O(1) auxiliary space. It is an excellent oracle for small inputs but performs roughly n(n-1)/2 checks on large arrays.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Recursively split the original index range. Every pair belongs to exactly one of three categories: both indices in the left half, both in the right half, or one in each half. Recursive calls count the first two categories while returning sorted copies of the corresponding values. The third category can be counted before merging because every original left index precedes every original right index.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each left value in ascending order, advance a right pointer while \u003ccode>left_value &gt; 2 * right[pointer]\u003c/code>. The qualifying right values form a prefix because doubling preserves order. As left values increase, the pointer never needs to move backward, so its position is the number of qualifying right values for that left value. Summing those positions counts every cross pair exactly once in linear time. Then merge the sorted halves in the ordinary way for the caller's next level.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Counting and merging must be separate passes: the comparison used for sorted merging is \u003ccode>left &lt;= right\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>left &gt; 2 * right\u003c/code>. The former alone cannot decide the latter, especially for negative values. Induction over the recursion tree proves completeness: the three categories partition all pairs, and the cross sweep counts exactly the third.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [1, 3, 2, 3, 1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>split into original index halves [1, 3] and [2, 3, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Recursive results: left [1, 3] with 0 pairs\u003c/p>\u003cpre>right [1, 2, 3] with 1 pair from its own 3 before 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Cross count: left 1 qualifies with none; left 3 qualifies with right 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>add 1 cross pair; merge to [1, 1, 2, 3, 3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Total 0 + 1 + 1 = {{2}}\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The sorted values in the trace represent original subranges, not a license to forget index order. Sorting a half is safe only after pairs entirely inside it have already been counted. A cross pair has the correct original index order regardless of the positions in the sorted copies.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def reverse_pairs(values):\n    def solve(part):\n        if len(part) &lt;= 1:\n            return list(part), 0\n        mid = len(part) // 2\n        left, left_count = solve(part[:mid])\n        right, right_count = solve(part[mid:])\n        count = left_count + right_count\n        j = 0\n        for value in left:\n            while j &lt; len(right) and value &gt; 2 * right[j]:\n                j += 1\n            count += j\n        merged = []\n        i = j = 0\n        while i &lt; len(left) and j &lt; len(right):\n            if left[i] &lt;= right[j]:\n                merged.append(left[i])\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                merged.append(right[j])\n                j += 1\n        merged.extend(left[i:])\n        merged.extend(right[j:])\n        return merged, count\n\n    return solve(values)[1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At each recursion level, cross counting, slicing, and merging process O(n) values. With O(log n) levels, time is O(n log n). Peak live slices and merged lists use O(n) auxiliary space, plus O(log n) recursion frames. This is a peak-space claim, not a claim that no intermediate lists are allocated.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code> rather than \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code>; equality with twice the right value is not a qualifying pair.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not reset the right pointer for every left value, which would make the cross pass quadratic.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not test only ordinary inversions: negative numbers can reverse the intuition about doubling.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In fixed-width languages, widen before multiplying by two and store the pair count in a sufficiently wide type.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorting the entire array before counting destroys the original index-order condition.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count ordinary inversions by counting left-side suffixes during the merge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace the factor 2 by a positive constant and keep the monotone sweep; a negative factor requires a different ordering argument.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use coordinate compression and a Fenwick tree to count earlier values above each threshold while scanning the original array.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:324":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rearrange the given numbers in place into the strict pattern \u003ccode>a[0] &lt; a[1] &gt; a[2] &lt; a[3] &gt; ...\u003c/code>. The result must preserve all occurrences. For \u003ccode>[1, 5, 1, 1, 6, 4]\u003c/code>, one valid result is \u003ccode>[1, 6, 1, 5, 1, 4]\u003c/code>. The original task guarantees that some valid arrangement exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Equal values are allowed, but equality is forbidden between adjacent positions. A single element is already valid. We assume a feasible input rather than detecting impossibility; for example, no algorithm can make \u003ccode>[1, 1]\u003c/code> strict. The task permits a sorted-copy construction; a more involved expected-linear selection and virtual-index partition is an alternative.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reorder so that nums[0] &lt; nums[1] &gt; nums[2] &lt; nums[3]..., with repeated values allowed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Split the values into a small half and a large half. Where must copies of the median go so that they never touch?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Partitioning and quicksort\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort a copy; fill the even indices with the small half in reverse and the odd indices with the large half in reverse, so copies of the median end up at opposite ends and never become neighbours. The O(n) time version finds the median by quickselect and places values with a three-way partition over \"virtual\" indices (1, 3, 5, ..., 0, 2, 4, ...).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time and O(n) space with sorting; O(n) expected time, O(1) extra space with quickselect and virtual indexing\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate permutations until one satisfies every neighboring inequality. In the worst case this examines O(n!) permutations, each requiring O(n) checks; storing a candidate uses O(n) space. Duplicates make many permutations equivalent but do not eliminate the factorial worst case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort the values, split them into a lower half of ceil(n/2) values and an upper half of floor(n/2), then fill even positions from the lower half in reverse and odd positions from the upper half in reverse. Peaks come from the upper half; valleys come from the lower half. Reversing both halves spreads equal boundary values apart instead of lining them up in adjacent positions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why strict? Write the sorted values as \u003ccode>s[0..n-1]\u003c/code>, and let \u003ccode>m=ceil(n/2)\u003c/code>. For peak number j the constructed value is \u003ccode>s[n-1-j]\u003c/code>; its left valley is \u003ccode>s[m-1-j]\u003c/code>, and, if present, its right valley is \u003ccode>s[m-2-j]\u003c/code>. If the left comparison failed, the sorted interval between those indices would consist of one repeated value. For even n=2k, that gives k+1 equal copies, more than the k nonadjacent positions available. For odd n=2k+1, it gives k+1 copies; these would have to occupy all even positions, leaving only larger values at the intervening peaks. But the equal interval starts at index k-j, which is positive for every peak, so at least one other sorted value is no larger than those copies. That contradicts feasibility. The right valley is no larger than the left valley, making its comparison strict too.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [1, 5, 1, 1, 6, 4]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sorted [1, 1, 1, 4, 5, 6]; split [1, 1, 1] | [4, 5, 6]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse each half for placement\u003c/p>\u003cpre>valleys [1, 1, 1], peaks [6, 5, 4]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Interleave valley then peak\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result \u003cmark>[1, 6, 1, 5, 1, 4]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def wiggle_sort(values):\n    ordered = sorted(values)\n    middle = (len(values) + 1) // 2\n    values[::2] = ordered[:middle][::-1]\n    values[1::2] = ordered[middle:][::-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting takes O(n log n) time; slicing and assignment take O(n). The sorted copy and slices require O(n) auxiliary space. The function modifies the supplied list and returns \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Alternating the two halves in forward order can place equal boundary values next to each other.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A non-strict \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> check does not satisfy the requested wiggle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use ceil(n/2) lower-half entries because even positions are more numerous when n is odd.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Without the feasibility promise, a strict arrangement may not exist; this function does not report that situation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use quickselect for a median and three-way partition through virtual indices for expected O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary partition space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return a new arrangement instead of modifying the input list.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:hr:ctci-merge-sort":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the adjacent swaps performed by insertion sort on a list of integers. Insertion sort moves an element left while it is strictly smaller than its predecessor. For \u003ccode>[2, 1, 3, 1]\u003c/code>, the count is 3. Compute each test case independently; the function below returns one case's count and leaves its input unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Lists can contain duplicates, negative integers, and up to many thousands of elements. Equal values are not swapped by the stated stable insertion procedure. The answer can be as large as n(n-1)/2, exceeding a 32-bit signed integer; Python integers grow as needed. Empty and single-element inputs have zero swaps.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count how many adjacent swaps insertion sort would make, for arrays of up to 100,000 elements and several test cases.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each adjacent swap fixes exactly one pair that is out of order. How can merge sort count those pairs in bulk?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Merge sort and counting during the merge\u003c/p>\u003cp>The answer is the number of inversions. During merge sort, whenever an element is taken from the right half while left[i:] remains, add len(left) - i. Ties must take from the left, or equal values are counted as inversions. Use Python ints (the total can exceed 2^31).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Simulate insertion sort and increment a counter on every adjacent swap. It is O(n²) in the worst case and O(n) space if a copy is used. Alternatively directly test every pair of indices. Both are useful small-input checks but too slow for large reverse-ordered cases.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The answer equals the number of inversions: pairs \u003ccode>i &lt; j\u003c/code> whose left value is strictly greater than the right value. Swapping adjacent out-of-order values removes exactly their mutual inversion and does not change their order relative to any third element. The sorted final list has no inversions, so insertion sort performs exactly one swap per initial inversion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count those pairs using merge sort. Recursively count inversions within each original half, then merge the sorted halves. If the next right value is smaller than the next left value, it is also smaller than every remaining left value. Add the entire remaining left suffix length. If the values tie, take the left first so equal values do not count as inversions. Every cross pair is counted exactly when its right value is emitted, and every pair belongs to exactly one recursive split.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [2, 1, 3, 1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left [2, 1] has 1 inversion; right [3, 1] has 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sorted halves [1, 2] and [1, 3]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>take left 1 on the tie; then right 1 is smaller than left 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 1 cross inversion, then finish the merge\u003c/p>\u003cpre>total 1 + 1 + 1 = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def insertion_swap_count(values):\n    def solve(part):\n        if len(part) &lt;= 1:\n            return list(part), 0\n        mid = len(part) // 2\n        left, left_count = solve(part[:mid])\n        right, right_count = solve(part[mid:])\n        count = left_count + right_count\n        merged = []\n        i = j = 0\n        while i &lt; len(left) and j &lt; len(right):\n            if left[i] &lt;= right[j]:\n                merged.append(left[i])\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                count += len(left) - i\n                merged.append(right[j])\n                j += 1\n        merged.extend(left[i:])\n        merged.extend(right[j:])\n        return merged, count\n\n    return solve(values)[1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The merge and slicing work per level is O(n), and there are O(log n) levels, yielding O(n log n) time. Peak auxiliary storage for live slices and merged lists is O(n), with O(log n) recursion frames. Python's integer count does not overflow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Counting equal values as inversions overcounts swaps; choose the left value on ties.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Adding only one when taking a smaller right value misses the rest of the left suffix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorting before counting loses original index order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reset state for each test case; the displayed function keeps the counter local to each call.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count inversions online with coordinate compression and a Fenwick tree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return both the sorted list and the count when downstream work needs the sorted values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
